# The Struggle for Rights: A Scene of Victory!
Tamil title: உரிமைப் போர் - வெற்றிக் காட்சி!

“*Arya Mayai*! *Arya Mayai*!” they cried.

“What is this *Arya Mayai*?” people asked as they gathered. Calling, “Listen, elders,” the speakers turned the handwritten pages in their hands and read aloud. “This is the Aryan illusion,” they concluded.

Applause and cheers arose among the assembled people. The reader set out from there and walked away. The crowd did not disperse but went along with him. After walking a little distance, the comrade stopped and began, “Dear comrades!” The crowd grew larger. The people did not openly demand, “Read it! Read again what you read there!” Yet the speed with which they pressed together and gathered said precisely that.

He read, holding the manuscript.

The people listened.

“This, comrades, is *Arya Mayai*. This is what the government banned,” the reader said, and delivered a speech.

It was morning, 9:30, the hour when the fierce heat began. Even so, the people eagerly heard him speak.

He finished with the movement's flag in one hand and the text of *Arya Mayai* in the other.

Again came applause and the cry, “Long live! Long live!”

The volunteer moved on, and the crowd followed.

This is the scene we witnessed on the first of last month in North Arcot district. Not in one or two places, but in more than a hundred towns, such scenes could be seen.

Small and large towns alike appeared capable of captivating thought that day.

Following a decision by the district DMK organisation, the talk everywhere was of “*Arya Mayai*! *Arya Mayai*!”

Holding handwritten sheets, hundreds of movement comrades read and explained *Arya Mayai* in public places that day.

When we saw those heroic young bulls holding proudly aloft the movement's black-and-red flag and raising valiant slogans, our whole heart swelled. A feeling overwhelmed us as though we no longer walked upon the earth but floated in the sky.

Not in one or two places: the struggle for freedom of writing extended across an entire district, and hundreds of volunteers took part. Look at the list. It has not been included because it would add pages to the book.

At 105 places, according to the information available to us, the struggle for freedom of writing took place that day.

Not secretly, but openly!

The heroes of North Arcot entered the struggle after sending information to the officers in every town and their superiors.

“At nine in the morning on 1 March 1951, at such-and-such a place, I shall read the government-banned book *Arya Mayai*.” The movement's branches throughout the district gave the government a week's advance notice in this form and then joined the struggle for freedom of writing.

They informed the authorities beforehand.

There was no disturbance.

It was conducted peacefully.

Our comrades and heroes broke the backbone of the rulers' autocracy!

At the places where the struggle occurred, Congress despotism did not operate. No one was arrested. Every official who arrived cooperated in enabling the people to sit in order and hear *Arya Mayai*. Would the officers themselves have had no desire to learn what it contained?

North Arcot district has conducted the struggle for freedom of writing successfully.

It was not secret. Through statements, notices and letters, the rulers and the public were informed in advance.

The government's autocracy was reduced to dust!

Our young bulls have delivered a proper lash to despotic rule.

We did not create this situation. The excesses of the rulers brought us to the point of violating the ban.

For everything, the people raise the longing cry, “There is none! There is none!” Storm, poverty, hunger and death rage through life; the country lies subjected to such strange agony.

The government that ought to remove this pain instead plays the veena and repeatedly hurls spears at the people.

The people are like a hungry tiger, and spears are played upon them!

Under Congress rule, which raised sky-high cries of “Self-rule! Freedom!”, the practice of punishing writing and criminalising speech continues to increase.

This “frenzy” arises naturally and easily among those possessed by the arrogance of power. But these disciples of Gandhi once roared countless times that such frenzy must not exist. The people therefore expected that frenzy would have no place under self-rule and wisdom would govern. Yet frenzy did not disappear; instead, it continues to grow.

Because of this autocratic obsession that “everything must occur solely according to my wish,” not one or two but many spears keep flying at us as we perform rationalist work.

We who carry out rationalist work existed under the white man's rule too. We performed the same work then as now. We held meetings, spoke, wrote and staged plays.

Yet even then we did not suffer hardships of this kind.

A foreigner ruled, and still we were neither attacked nor obstructed.

But now? Our own people rule. It is self-rule! And under it spears are thrown at us and arrows of repression fly!

After Congress members obtained the “good fortune” of ascending the seat of power, they imposed many forms of repression upon our movement:

“A play? Do not perform it!”

“A meeting? No permission!”

“Are you writing a book? I shall ban it!”

They have continued threatening us in this manner.

Through the sacred spectacle staged by the rulers over *Arya Mayai*, the country saw the frenzy that had arisen among Congress rulers.

For the single reason that we are an opposition party, the government continuously imposes prohibitions upon us.

Freedom of speech, freedom of writing and freedom of thought: every person born human must possess all three.

But under this government there is no room for these three to move about alive.

They are humanity's fundamental freedoms.

Even these have no path under self-rule.

Why? We began asking this question.

“Why do you impose Section 144 upon a meeting?”

“Why do you ban a book?”

“Why do you say a play must not be performed?”

We asked the government. No answer came. The government displayed its clubs and guns.

We appealed with affection: “Sir, is this conduct proper?”

“Who are you to question us?” Congress rule threatened.

Only after seeing this autocratic frenzy spread did we reach a decision.

We declared that we could not lose our fundamental rights.

Following resolutions to safeguard freedom of speech and struggle for freedom of writing, we decided to display our condemnation.

We made this decision openly and announced it. The people welcomed it.

Through public meetings vast as the sea, the people's assembly said: “Yes, autocracy continues to grow. For every matter this government raises the rod; it has no magnanimity.”

The people also conducted our struggle for rights with enthusiasm.

Our heroes have acquired the distinction of being messengers of justice.

Singing the heroic strain, “The law may strike; we do not care,” they have fought to safeguard freedom of writing.

Their action is a treasure for the Dravidian land yearning for liberation.

The cry they raised on 1 March 1951 is a warning to autocratic government.

The awakening that blossomed among the people that day with the cry “*Arya Mayai*! *Arya Mayai*!” is a medal for our victory.

“Speaking and writing are crimes. If you disobey, I shall enact a law and stop you,” the government threatens.

“What if you violate the ban?” justice asks.

“I shall throw the law at you and punish you,” frenzy threatens.

“Very well. It does not matter. I am going to fight for justice,” rights reply.

“You will fight? My guns will teach you wisdom,” autocracy says, pointing to its weapons with its eyes.

The courageous lions who set out to fight for justice line up and present their chests.

What can the government do? It is frightened. Confusedly asking, “What shall we do?”, it retreats.

North Arcot has displayed this scene.

Autocracy threatens only while it believes the people will tremble at its voice.

Through North Arcot, the truth has become clear that it becomes frightened once it knows the people will defy it.

“No one must read or hear it.” That was the reason for the ban on *Arya Mayai*.

“We shall spread it widely enough for everyone to hear!” That was the declaration of those who joined the moral struggle of 1 March 1951.

The people read it.

The people defied the ban!

But the government that had threatened them did not do what it said it would. The heroes now move about in the joy of victory.

Despotic rule lies with its head bowed in shame.

Every government that walks the path of autocracy will reach this same stage of humiliation.

Not only North Arcot but world history itself says so.

Yet Congress rulers behave as though they do not understand this truth: they roar when frenzy rises, and when pain comes as the consequence of that roar, they bend and hide.

A shameless condition!

Every government that takes away rights receives this “shameless condition” as its reward.

The cry for rights raised by the warriors of the North Arcot district DMK has taught this authoritarian government a fine lesson. We congratulate the heroes who accomplished such a difficult deed. We offer them the affection of the Dravidian land.

Long live the warriors of rights! May the thirst for rights grow!

(*Dravida Nadu*, 18 March 1951)

---NOTES---
`ஆரிய மாயை` (*Arya Mayai*, “The Aryan Illusion”) was the banned work read publicly during this campaign. The notice date appears once in OCR as `7-3-57`, but the article repeatedly identifies the action as 1 March 1951 and was published on 18 March 1951; the translation uses the internally confirmed date while preserving the OCR source. The damaged climax `தீடுத்தது மக்கள்!` is rendered “The people read it” from the immediately preceding ban on reading and the repeated description of public readings. `அறப்போர்` is rendered “moral struggle,” denoting disciplined nonviolent civil resistance.
